@page page_glossary_home Glossary

@tableofcontents

This page collects short, precise definitions of terms that recur throughout
Peano's documentation. Where a term originates from a particular publication,
the entry says so and points to the source.


# Spacetree

A **spacetree** is the tree-based adaptive Cartesian mesh data structure
underlying Peano. The term is used as a generalisation of the classical
quadtree (2D) and octree (3D) data structures to arbitrary dimension @f$
d \geq 2 @f$ and to partitionings other than bipartitioning.

## Construction

The computational domain is scaled and dilated to fit into a unit hypercube
of dimension @f$ d @f$. This hypercube is cut into @f$ k @f$ equidistant
slices along each coordinate axis, yielding @f$ k^d @f$ small cubes that form
a regular Cartesian grid; the cut also defines a parent-child relation
between the newly created cubes and the cube they were cut from. This cutting
is continued recursively and independently for each new cube. The process
terminates in a set of cubes @f$ \mathcal{T} @f$ whose leaves, i.e. the cubes
that are not refined any further, span an adaptive Cartesian grid, while the
parent-child relation (or its inverse) imposes a hierarchy, i.e. a tree, on
@f$ \mathcal{T} @f$.

Equivalently, if the level @f$ \ell @f$ of a tree node is defined as the
length of the path from the root (the original bounding box) to that node,
the nodes of one level span a regular grid @f$ \Omega_{h,\ell} @f$, and the
adaptive grid as a whole is @f$ \Omega_h = \bigcup_\ell \Omega_{h,\ell} @f$.
Tree language and grid language for spacetrees are therefore equivalent, once
an adaptive Cartesian grid is read as a composite of disconnected regular
Cartesian grids of different resolution, embedded into each other.

Peano tripartitions, i.e. it uses @f$ k = 3 @f$, and enumerates/linearises
the cells of a spacetree along a Peano space-filling curve rather than via
plain depth-first or breadth-first search, since a space-filling-curve-based
enumeration turns the tree's parent-child partial order into a total order
and yields, among other properties, face-connected traversal steps.

@image html documentation/glossary-spacetree-figure1.png "Figure 1 from Weinzierl (2019): construction of a spacetree from repeated regular refinement of a square, and the resulting Peano space-filling curve traversal."

<i>Figure reproduced from: T. Weinzierl, "The Peano Software&mdash;Parallel,
Automaton-based, Dynamically Adaptive Grid Traversals," ACM Transactions on
Mathematical Software, 45(2), Article 14, 2019,
<a href="https://doi.org/10.1145/3319797">doi:10.1145/3319797</a>
(<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.04496">arXiv:1506.04496</a>).</i>

The four panels of the figure, left to right, show: the computational domain
embedded into a square; the Peano space-filling curve motif inducing an
ordering on the @f$ 3 \times 3 @f$ Cartesian grid obtained from one
refinement step of that square; the same construction after a further
refinement step, still following the Peano curve; and, on top, an adaptively
refined Cartesian grid with, below it, the corresponding spacetree.


*/
